---
/**
 * The mark.
 *
 * WHAT THE EXPORT ACTUALLY ANIMATES, measured rather than read off its script:
 *   - transform is matrix(1,0,0,1,0,0) at every scroll position AND on hover,
 *     so there is no rotation and no scale. The rotate(45deg) and scale(.92)
 *     in the script are dead code from an older version.
 *   - the conic ring element exists but sits at opacity 0 both at rest and on
 *     hover, so it never paints. It was added here anyway as a scroll-progress
 *     indicator and has now been REMOVED: on a light ground it read as a pale
 *     circle behind the mark, which is the badge that was explicitly cut. The
 *     export leaves it invisible for a reason. Do not add it back.
 *   - the motion is COLOUR: `transition: filter .5s ease` on the mark and
 *     `background .45s ease, box-shadow .45s ease` on the chip, with the mark
 *     going brightness(0) on hover.
 *
 * So the mark fades between inks over half a second. That is the whole effect,
 * and shortening it to 250ms is what made the logo feel dead.
 *
 * This is the REAL brand mark from design/prototypes/brand/, not the two
 * circles that stood in for it. The stand-in was never meant to ship; the
 * asset was in the repo the whole time and nothing pointed at it.
 *
 * The mark NEVER rotates and carries NO badge. Measured off the export at
 * five scroll positions — hero, about, services, projects, footer — the
 * computed transform is matrix(1,0,0,1,0,0) every time. The rotate(45deg)
 * and scale(.92) in the prototype's script are dead code from an older
 * version: reading that source describes behaviour the export does not use.
 *
 * Colour is the only thing that changes: black on light grounds, yellow on
 * dark. On the yellow hero that means a bare black mark directly on the
 * yellow, with no circle behind it.
 */
import mark from '../assets/roars-mark.png'
---

<a class="chip" href="/" data-logo-chip aria-label="Roars, home">
  {/* The scroll-progress ring. A conic gradient with its centre masked out,
      so it is an ANNULUS — the previous attempt read as a filled disc, which
      is the badge that was cut. Driven from src/scripts/topbar.ts. */}
  <span class="chip__ring" data-logo-ring aria-hidden="true"></span>
  {/* Masked, not <img>: the mark has to take a token colour — accent over the
      hero, ink over light grounds, white on dark — and a raster cannot. The
      mask keeps one asset and lets `background` do the colouring. */}
  <span
    class="chip__mark"
    data-logo-mark
    style={`--mark:url(${mark.src})`}
    aria-hidden="true"
  ></span>
</a>

<style>
  .chip {
    position: relative;
    transition: background 0.45s ease, box-shadow 0.45s ease;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    margin-left: -9px;
    border-radius: var(--r-circle);
    color: var(--c-accent);
    /* The export's own intro: a quarter turn and a slight overshoot as the
       page opens, 1100ms on a 300ms delay. It settles to nothing, so it costs
       one animation and no layout. On the chip rather than the mark so the
       pulse can own the mark's `animation` outright — see the note there.
       The mark is centred in this 52px box, so scaling and rotating the box
       puts it through exactly the same arc it went through before. */
    animation: logoIntro 1100ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) 300ms both;
  }
  /* Appears once the page has actually moved, so it reads as progress rather
     than decoration sitting there at zero.

     The two masks are the whole point. Without them the conic gradient fills
     the 52px circle and the mark sits on a pale yellow plate — which is
     exactly how this shipped last time and exactly what had been cut. The
     inner 66% is punched out, leaving a 3px band clear of the 33px mark. */
  .chip__ring {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    border-radius: var(--r-circle);
    background: conic-gradient(
      from 0turn,
      var(--c-accent) var(--p, 0turn),
      rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.16) var(--p, 0turn)
    );
    -webkit-mask: radial-gradient(circle, #0000 0 66%, #000 68%);
    mask: radial-gradient(circle, #0000 0 66%, #000 68%);
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.45s ease;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* THE MARK IS ONE COLOUR AT ALL TIMES. It does not interpolate.
     Transitioning the ink meant half a second of values between yellow and
     black — rgb(151,127,5) and the like — and a dark gold at 26px reads as
     red-brown, which is what was showing on every ground change. Moving the
     interpolation to oklab held the hue but did not fix it, because the
     problem is the intermediate value existing at all, not its hue.
     So the ink SWITCHES. The mark is #FFD400, #0B0B0B or #FFFFFF and nothing
     between. The motion is the progress ring and the intro; the ink is a
     state, and states do not need to be animated to feel alive.
     `--mark-ink` stays a registered property so it holds a real colour value
     rather than a string. */
  .chip__mark {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: 26px;
    height: 33px;
    --mark-ink: var(--c-accent);
    background: var(--mark-ink);
    -webkit-mask: var(--mark) center / contain no-repeat;
    mask: var(--mark) center / contain no-repeat;
    /* No transition on the ink. See the note above. */
    /* The intro animation is on .chip, NOT here. Both it and the pulse want
       the `animation` property, and one element cannot hold two of those from
       two rules: the pulse would replace the intro, and removing the pulse
       class at the end would set the intro running again — a logo that
       re-introduces itself every time you cross a section. They are on
       separate elements so neither can touch the other. */
  }

  @keyframes logoIntro {
    0%   { transform: scale(0.2) rotate(-90deg); opacity: 0; }
    62%  { transform: scale(1.12) rotate(8deg); opacity: 1; }
    100% { transform: scale(1) rotate(0deg); opacity: 1; }
  }

  /* Light ground. The observer sets this on the top bar. */

  :global(.topbar.is-light) .chip__mark { --mark-ink: var(--c-footer); }
  /* brightness(0) in the export: the mark goes to ink on hover, from either
     ground, over the same half second. */
  .chip:hover .chip__mark { --mark-ink: var(--c-footer); }
  :global(.topbar:not(.is-light)) .chip:hover .chip__mark { --mark-ink: var(--c-white); }

  /* THE SECTION-CHANGE PULSE.
     A 180ms flash of pink, a second of yellow, then back to whatever ink the
     section it has arrived on calls for. src/scripts/topbar.ts adds the class
     each time a new section crosses the middle of the viewport and takes it
     off when the animation ends.

     THE SPLIT, AND HOW TO CHANGE IT. Keyframe offsets have to be literal
     percentages — a custom property cannot be used for one — so the two
     durations are written here and the percentage is derived by hand:

         pink    180ms
         yellow 1000ms
         total  1180ms   -> the boundary is 180/1180 = 15.25%

     Move either number and the boundary has to be recomputed with it. Pink
     was a full second at first and read as a colour change rather than a
     flash; at 180ms it registers without the logo looking like it has gone
     pink for a living.

     HELD STOPS, NOT A FADE. The note above records that transitioning this
     ink was tried and pulled: the values between yellow and black come out
     around rgb(151,127,5), and a dark gold at 26px reads as red-brown. Pink
     to yellow blends through orange and is no better. steps(1, end) means the
     mark never holds an in-between value at all — it shows pink, then yellow,
     then the ground ink, and nothing else. That is the same rule the earlier
     note set, kept rather than worked around.

     background-color, not --mark-ink: animating a custom property needs it
     registered through @property before it will interpolate as a colour, and
     painting the value directly does the same job with nothing to register.
     When the class comes off, the element falls back to its own
     `background: var(--mark-ink)` and lands on the right ground ink by
     itself — including white, on a dark section. */
  @keyframes logoPulse {
    0%,     15.24% { background-color: var(--c-pulse); }
    15.25%, 99.99% { background-color: var(--c-accent); }
    100%           { background-color: var(--mark-ink); }
  }

  .chip__mark.is-pulsing {
    animation: logoPulse 1180ms steps(1, end) 1 both;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .chip__mark { transition: none; }
    /* Decoration carrying no information, so it does not run. topbar.ts tests
       the same query and never adds the class; this is the belt to that
       brace, for a preference switched on after the page loaded. */
    .chip__mark.is-pulsing { animation: none; }
  }
</style>
